        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 765,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:01:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 823,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 766,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124227,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 766,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>1E80 Common warts</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:01:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 824,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 767,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124228,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 767,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> common viral wart.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:01:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 825,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 768,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124229,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:01:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124230,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The hands and fingers are common sites of occurrence {22131115}. The elbows, knees, flexures, face, and genitalia are among other reported locations {22131115; 9666831}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:01:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124231,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lesions present as single or multiple verrucous, keratotic papules or nodules, 1&ndash;20&nbsp;mm in diameter {22131115; 9666831}. Facial lesions may appear filiform {1647902}. Occasional verrucae present as a cutaneous horn.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:01:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124232,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The reported prevalence rates are as high as 16% in children and 3.5% in adults {1647901; 9666831}. Immunosuppression is a risk factor {22131115}. Verrucae have been reported to account for 10% of skin lesions in renal transplant recipients {16182734}. Lesions may occur on tattoos, and on the scalp in a possible association with barber clippers {34646630; 32315659}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:01:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124233,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Verruca vulgaris is caused by various subtypes of HPV. The main HPV types implicated are HPV1, HPV2, and HPV4 {22131115}. Others include HPV7, and HPV26&ndash;29 {2156916}. HPV75&ndash;77 have been detected in immunosuppressed patients {9146528}. The genital HPV types HPV6 and HPV11 have rarely been detected in common warts among children {9185912}. HPV16 (a genital type with oncogenic potential) was detected in 6.6% of non-genital warts in 45&nbsp;immunocompetent patients {17083881}. Verrucae sometimes occur on the vulva in children and adults {17414099}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:01:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124234,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>HPV infects the suprabasal keratinocytes, the interappendageal epidermis, and the stem cells of the follicular bulge {26193301}. HPV DNA synthesis takes place in the superficial prickle cell layer. Full virus assembly with capsid production occurs within the accentuated granular cell layer. Viral particles are released via desquamated cornified cells {2156916}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:01:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124235,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Excised lesions mirror the clinical appearances.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:01:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124236,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "<p>The lesions feature hyperkeratosis, acanthosis, and papillomatosis, with prominent, inwardly proliferating rete ridges. Columns of parakeratosis overlie foci of papillomatosis. There is hypergranulosis, with coarse keratohyalin granules. Koilocytes are often present in the superficial epidermis {22131115; 23932731}. Dilated, tortuous capillaries occupy the papillary dermis. A lichenoid inflammatory infiltrate is sometimes present and may correlate with regression {7430479}.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>Verrucae should be distinguished from seborrhoeic keratoses, verrucous psoriasis, and epidermolytic acanthomas {19942951; 15900122; 33911795}; this is particularly challenging when koilocytes are absent, which is more common in older warts. Occasionally, distinction from an inflamed seborrhoeic keratosis is not possible, leading to the use of terms such as &ldquo;verruciform keratosis&rdquo; or &ldquo;benign squamous papilloma&rdquo;. Keratoacanthoma shares architectural features with crateriform verruca {26970425}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:01:58"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 775,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124237,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 775,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:01:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 833,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 776,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124238,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 776,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Routine HPV subtyping is not indicated.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:01:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 836,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 779,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124239,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 779,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> single or multiple verrucous, keratotic papules or nodules; papillomatosis; parakeratotic columns; hypergranulosis.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> koilocytosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:01:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 834,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 777,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124240,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 777,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:01:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 835,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 778,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124241,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 10,
        "headingId": 778,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Spontaneous involution may occur {9666831}, although it is less frequent in immunocompromised patients {22131115}. In patients infected with HIV, antiretroviral therapy often expedites regression {11903251}. Squamous dysplasia occasionally supervenes {2540085; 12142612}. Rare verrucae may progress to squamous cell carcinoma, especially in the setting of immunosuppression {7949492}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:01:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]